{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-122542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/62f33dab4305a41a0bc180231fef7015361dec4aac63f14c31fd48a39b8fcd68.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ traversed between the points $(2,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},6),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{t}^{4}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4x{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4(\\frac{2}{t})(t)^{2} \\\\  & =8t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}8t\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fca31deb9c10cb193e0be0529362eb96638e0748dd6d217885ed0dc7ce808b10"} {"question" "q-122542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $3x=y$ traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,9).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3x=y\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{3},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 9\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 9$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{3})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9{(\\frac{t}{3})}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =9(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9})(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fca31deb9c10cb193e0be0529362eb96638e0748dd6d217885ed0dc7ce808b10"} {"question" "q-122530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-{t}^{3}"] ["math" "3{t}^{2}-1"] ["math" "{t}^{2}+1"] ["math" "{t}^{3}"] ["math" "{t}^{3}+3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{t}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-4,2).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2-3t,\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fca31deb9c10cb193e0be0529362eb96638e0748dd6d217885ed0dc7ce808b10"} {"question" "q-122527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-3t"] ["math" "2-5t"] ["math" "2t+5"] ["math" "4-9t"] ["math" "4t-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4-9t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/2860103dc3d1019cddcffa504b3cce1fb8a84cfd046bc796712d1e16516d5269.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,e),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,⟹\\,x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4y{e}^{2x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4y{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & =4t({t}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fca31deb9c10cb193e0be0529362eb96638e0748dd6d217885ed0dc7ce808b10"} {"question" "q-149714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/6cabe5598368b4f26ccca515d4f4011100e0bc410b72d919db9681b3145814fb.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,{e}^{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)={e}^{x}{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}){t}^{2} \\\\  & =(t){t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fca31deb9c10cb193e0be0529362eb96638e0748dd6d217885ed0dc7ce808b10"} {"question" "q-122534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/02582b9146af7bfa73ade5c4bd26ec7102650f324fede161f98482153730e868.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x={y}^{2}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(6,\\sqrt{6}),$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =12{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{25}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =125-5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =5(25-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fca31deb9c10cb193e0be0529362eb96638e0748dd6d217885ed0dc7ce808b10"} {"question" "q-122574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "25-\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "36-\\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "5(25-\\sqrt{5})"] ["math" "6(36-\\sqrt{6})"] ["math" "6-\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5(25-\\sqrt{5})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola ${y}^{2}=8x$ from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8},3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{4}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{t}{4})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{24x}{y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{24x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot (\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})}{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3t\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+16,$ $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({25}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fca31deb9c10cb193e0be0529362eb96638e0748dd6d217885ed0dc7ce808b10"} {"question" "q-122271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{47}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{51}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{53}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{61}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{67}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{61}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x={e}^{y/2}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={e}^{t/2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t/2},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t/2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y} \\\\  & =6{({e}^{t/2})}^{6}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}6{e}^{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={e}^{t}+4,$ $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2({6}^{3/2}-{5}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(6\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fca31deb9c10cb193e0be0529362eb96638e0748dd6d217885ed0dc7ce808b10"} {"question" "q-149745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{6}-6\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "6\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/46f2f23c1cc2cb2caacd3703665af673a52a7e876665c7ba9420ae72138e1068.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(1,2),$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+4}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x{y}^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\cdot {t}^{6} \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{4}+4,$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{20}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\sqrt{5}-5\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fca31deb9c10cb193e0be0529362eb96638e0748dd6d217885ed0dc7ce808b10"} {"question" "q-122593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{21\\sqrt{3}}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{35}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{49}{15}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-122593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/8d060e257d74e6e0e6b7f35bfb89e9e974499059ddf4d029c0cace195ba67c13.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2y-4$ from $(0,2)$ to $(2,3),$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2t-4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t-4,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(2t-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4-t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fca31deb9c10cb193e0be0529362eb96638e0748dd6d217885ed0dc7ce808b10"} {"question" "q-149727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "q-149727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/state.json" "file_sha256" "6701e5a3f54f9b2c88ac408aa5e36d776875acbf79e32d614130a4475ba6cbf1"} {"question" "e-9435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/2e4e808d8264685f9b88c5b0052870174d8be3ed2a7195149d45552118186a49.png)\n\nBy setting $y = t$ where $t$ is a parameter, find a definite integral with respect to $t$ that is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\, \\textrm ds,$ where $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x = y^2$ traversed between the points $(1,1)$ and $(9 , 3),$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/example-9435.json" "file_sha256" "700b022cd4666b461ca907d4eeec153033fdd3495026be48fe5c7d5fd61da118"} {"question" "e-9435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y = t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2 , \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/example-9435.json" "file_sha256" "700b022cd4666b461ca907d4eeec153033fdd3495026be48fe5c7d5fd61da118"} {"question" "e-9463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/d0cebdd804ff244d037e14df6ec33efb86b522644f7b4addc688e27fd6a57e95.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} y^3e^{-x} \\: \\textrm{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\ln{y}$ from $(0, 1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac32\\ln 2 , 2\\sqrt{2}\\right),$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/example-9463.json" "file_sha256" "f850bf121e63173136f8d783ac389d709580c9df72c222a0a5e2412a1de24e8e"} {"question" "e-9463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = y^3e^{-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{3}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u = t^2 + 1,$ $\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{2}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{9}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{27-2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/example-9463.json" "file_sha256" "f850bf121e63173136f8d783ac389d709580c9df72c222a0a5e2412a1de24e8e"}] "usage" {"e-9435" [] "q-122271" [] "e-9463" []} "basis" 1854 "retractions" [[17592186179894 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n\n$∫_(C)\\frac{24x}{y}ds$,\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} = 8x$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8}, 3)$."] [17592186179501 :question/problem "By setting $y = t$ where $t$ is a parameter, find a definite integral with respect to $t$ that is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\, \\textrm ds$, where $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x = y^2$ traversed between the points $(1,1)$ and $(9, 3)$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3689/Source/Images/a7065ee8203134b582bc36dc9392dd61.png)"] [17592186179501 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y)$. Therefore, by setting $y = t$, we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain\n\n$$\n1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nis defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= 2ti + j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2t)^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y}\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= \\frac{t^{2}}{t} \\\\\n&= t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{x}{y}ds &= ∫_{1}^{3}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179510 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} y^3e^{-x} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\ln{y}$ from $(0, 1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac32\\ln 2, 2\\sqrt{2}\\right)$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3689/Source/Images/8e10ad385bc31844a4b0f2bf68c467e8.png)"] [17592186179510 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y)$. Therefore, by setting $y=t$, we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain\n\n$$\n1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nis defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\ln t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t}i + j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{t})^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{t^{2}} + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2} + 1}{t^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{\\mid t \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = y^3e^{-x}\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= t^{3}e^{-\\ln t} \\\\\n&= t^{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\\n&= t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)y^{3}e^{-x}ds &= ∫_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t^{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu = t^2 + 1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}dt &= ∫_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u^{3}} \\mid _{2}^{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{9^{3}} - \\sqrt{2^{3}}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{27 - 2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186177365 17592186177366 17592186177367 17592186177368 17592186177369] "immutable_fields" [[17592186177364 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177364 :answer-field/id] [17592186177364 :answer-field/key] [17